{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01CiMaTRNVH58htjPmHQC8hn","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me analyze the project context carefully and determine what to build next.\n\n## Project State Summary\n\n**Problem:** Classic Unix utilities lack formal specifications. Re-implementations diverge in observable behavior. This project derives specs from C source code, generates Go implementations from those specs using AI, and verifies parity via differential testing.\n\n**Architecture:**\n- `cmd/` - Go implementations of each utility\n- `pkg/testutils/` - Differential testing harness\n- `pkg/sys/` - Darwin/Linux syscall abstractions\n- `pkg/format/` - Shared output formatting\n- `magefiles/` - Build targets\n- `docs/` - Specifications (PRDs, use cases, test suites)\n\n\n- **Current implementations:** testutils and sys packages are complete with cross-platform support, format package has color, column, and size utilities, and I've got cat and du commands started with varying test coverage.\n- **Release roadmap:** The foundational infrastructure (batch 0) and individual command batches (cat, sponge, du) are all spec-complete but code hasn't started, with batch 2 beginning to appear in the pipeline.\n\nBut there's a discrepancy here—the source code shows that main.go files and utility packages already exist for cat, du, sponge, and several pkg modules, yet the analysis marks these as \"code not started.\" I need to reconcile what the constraint says about source code status versus what the analysis is reporting. Looking at the releases in scope, I can see that format, sys, and testutils already have implementations from the earlier batches. Cat and sponge are complete with tests, but du only has the main implementation without test coverage. Beyond that, the remaining work includes the trivial utilities batch and tee, both still pending their specifications.\n\nNow I'm checking the status of wc more carefully—the implementation status shows it's spec complete from an earlier release, but the code isn't actually present on this branch, and it's listed as \"not started\" in the analysis. Since I can only propose one task and must stay within the allowed releases, du's missing tests in release 01.3 seems like the most concrete incomplete work I can identify. Let me figure out the priority:\n\nRelease 00.0:\n- uc001-magefiles: excluded by DD7 (cmd/version is the only target, but magefiles itself is excluded). Actually wait, DD7 says \"The only code generation target in prd011-magefiles is cmd/version, which lives in cmd/version/.\" So cmd/version is a valid target. But we don't have source_code for it, and the spec status is spec_complete. However, no source code exists for cmd/version.\n- uc002-format: pkg/format already exists with code (color.go, column.go, size.go and tests)\n- uc003-sys: pkg/sys already exists with code\n- uc004-testutils: pkg/testutils already exists with code\n\nSo for 00.0, the question is whether cmd/version needs to be created\n\nLooking back at DD7, cmd/version is indeed the code generation target for prd011-magefiles, though I'm uncertain about the full PRD details. The analysis marks uc001-magefiles as \"not started,\" but since magefiles itself is excluded from generation, the real question is whether cmd/version should be built. Comparing what exists against what's needed, cmd/version appears to be missing for release 00.0, while du_test.go is missing for release 01.3.\n\nFor releases 02.0 through 02.2, the specs are still pending, so those can't move forward yet. Release 03.0 (cmd/wc) and 04.0/04.1 (cmd/ls) have complete specs but no code. So the earliest release with complete specs and missing code is either 00.0 (if cmd/version counts) or 01.3 (du_test.go).\n\nLooking at cmd/du specifically—the main.go file exists but there's no test file yet. I should verify that prd009-du exists and is complete before writing the tests, since that's the dependency requirement.\n\nFor the test file, I'll need to reference the PRD, the existing du implementation, and the testing utilities like difftest to understand the pattern. I should also check what the test suite structure looks like based on the rel01.3 naming convention to ensure consistency with the cat and sponge tests.\n\nLooking at how cat and sponge tests are structured, they follow a consistent pattern: TestMain compiles the binary, locates the reference implementation, and runs differential tests against it. The du test should do the same—build the du binary, find the gdu reference, and run the differential tests.\n\nSince the du implementation already covers the core requirements like recursive traversal, block size formatting, output format, symlink handling, and error cases, I need to design tests that verify each of these behaviors against the reference implementation.\n\nThe constraint limits me to 4 PRD sub-requirements per task, but the du implementation spans 8 sub-requirements total. I need to select which 4 are most critical to test—likely the core functionality like recursive traversal, block size display, output format, and symlink behavior, while potentially deferring error handling and argument independence to a separate test task. I'm settling on 4 as the right scope for a single task. For du, I'll focus on the essential requirements that define the tool's core behavior: recursive directory traversal with per-directory subtotals, converting to 1024-byte blocks, the SIZE\\tPATH output format, and handling multiple arguments with a default to the current directory. The symlink handling, error cases, and signal handling can wait for a follow-up task. Now I'll create the task definition with the du command and these four requirements.\n\nFor the test file, I need to set up the differential testing framework by reading the PRD, the existing du implementation, and the test utilities, then follow the pattern from cat_test.go. I'll use TestMain to build the binary and locate the GNU reference tool, skip tests if it's not available, and leverage the RunDiffTests utility for comparisons. I'll also need to create temporary directory structures to properly test the recursive traversal behavior.\n\nThe tests should cover recursive directory traversal with depth-first subtotals, block unit conversions from 512-byte to 1024-byte blocks, the SIZE\\tPATH output format, and handling of multiple arguments with a default to the current directory. The file needs to compile cleanly, pass all differential tests when gdu is installed, and follow the same structural pattern as the cat test reference.\n\nI should also check whether a test file already exists and verify the exact specification details, particularly around how the du binary name gets normalized since the GNU version uses the \"gdu\" prefix like Homebrew does.\n\nLooking at the analysis more carefully, du shows spec_complete status but codestatus is marked as \"not started\" with zero test files, even though cmd/du/main.go is listed in the source code. Following the constraint to trust the source code over documentation, the implementation exists but the tests don't.\n\nFor rel00.0, the analysis claims uc001-magefiles, uc002-format, uc003-sys, and uc004-testutils are all \"not started,\" but the source code shows format, sys, and testutils already have implementations. The analysis appears stale. The only genuinely missing piece is cmd/version for the magefiles use case, which DD7 identifies as the sole generation target. Since I don't have the PRD for cmd/version yet, I need to determine whether to prioritize this or focus on the stale analysis items first. and du has code but no tests yet. The clear next step is writing differential tests for du covering the main requirements. Let me verify the test spec exists in the docs—it should be marked spec_complete for that release—then I'll draft the task proposal focusing on those test cases.\n\nLooking at the PRD structure, du maps to prd009 with requirements organized as R1 for core traversal, R4 for error handling, and R5 for signals. I can scope this to exactly four sub-requirements—R1.1, R1.2, R1.3, and R1.5—which fits the constraint perfectly. The sponge test file already covers some of these like TestSpongePassthrough and TestSpongeWriteToFile, so I need to make sure the du tests don't duplicate that coverage. I'm identifying what the stitch agent needs to know to write these tests: the target file location, which existing code patterns to follow, the specific test cases required, the reference binary to compare against, and how to use the testing utilities. Now I'm drafting the task description in YAML format with all the necessary context about requirements, dependencies, and implementation details. The remaining requirements cover block unit conversion using ceiling division, output formatting with size and path separated by tabs, handling multiple arguments in order with a default to the current directory, and the design approach of building the du binary into a temp directory while locating the GNU reference binary via path lookup, skipping tests if the reference isn't available.\n\nFor the implementation, I'll use the existing test utilities for all comparisons rather than manual checks, normalize error messages by replacing the reference binary's prefix with \"du:\" so they match, and construct temporary directory structures with specific file content to ensure consistent block counts for differential testing. The acceptance criteria require the test file to compile cleanly, pass all differential tests when the reference binary is installed, and meet the specified requirements. I'm realizing there's a tension between the constraints—I can have 5-8 task requirements but only 4 PRD sub-requirements mapped to them. So I need to add more task-level requirements that aren't tied to specific PRD sub-requirements. Let me add a requirement about the test structure itself, like having a TestMain function that handles binary setup, and maybe a helper function for normalizing program names in output.\n\nThat gets me to 6 requirements total. I should also add another acceptance criterion about test isolation to support parallel execution, which brings me to 6 ACs. Let me verify the counts are balanced: 6 requirements, 4 design decisions, 6 acceptance criteria, and 4 PRD sub-requirements—all within the target ranges.\n\nNow looking at the source code for cmd/du/main.go, I see it covers more requirements than what I'm testing here. I'm focusing on R1.1 through R1.5 for this task, while R1.4, R4.1, R4.2, and R5.1 would be addressed in follow-up work. I need to make sure the task description is complete enough for the stitch agent to understand the package structure, that the reference binary is `gdu`, how to construct test directories, and what test pattern to use.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":81323,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8129,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"78efe284-a8d4-43a5-bb9f-f328cfcae0b3","uuid":"7e18400d-0316-4372-938a-000000000000"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01CiMaTRNVH58htjPmHQC8hn","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Project State Summary\n\n**Problem:** Classic Unix utilities lack formal specifications. This project derives specs from C source code, generates Go implementations via AI, and verifies parity through differential testing against GNU reference binaries.\n\n**Architecture:** Four-stage pipeline (extract → specify → synthesize → verify). Key components: `cmd/` (per-utility packages), `pkg/testutils/` (differential testing harness), `pkg/sys/` (syscall abstractions), `pkg/format/` (shared formatting), `magefiles/` (build targets), `docs/` (specifications).\n\n**Implementation State (from source_code):**\n- **Code exists:** pkg/testutils, pkg/sys, pkg/format, cmd/cat (+ tests), cmd/sponge (+ tests), cmd/du (implementation only — no test file)\n- **No code:** cmd/wc, cmd/ls, cmd/version, and all batch 2.x utilities\n\n**Current Priority:** Release 00.0 infrastructure packages have code. Releases 01.1 (cat) and 01.2 (sponge) have both implementation and tests. Release 01.3 (du) has implementation but **no differential test file** — this is the earliest incomplete deliverable.\n\n## Priority Reasoning\n\nRelease 01.3 is the earliest incomplete release. `cmd/du/main.go` exists but `cmd/du/du_test.go` does not (source_code confirms no test file; analysis shows testfiles: 0). Per P5 (dependency ordering), tests follow implementation. The du implementation is complete; the test file is the blocker for closing rel01.3.\n\n```yaml\n- index: 0\n  title: \"cmd/du: differential tests for prd009-du R1.1, R1.2, R1.3, R1.5\"\n  dependency: -1\n  description: |\n    deliverable_type: code\n\n    required_reading:\n      - docs/specs/product-requirements/prd009-du.yaml (du requirements R1.1-R1.5 covering core traversal, block units, output format, and argument handling)\n      - docs/specs/test-suites/test-rel-01.3.yaml (test cases for du use case)\n      - cmd/du/main.go (existing du implementation under test)\n      - pkg/testutils/difftest.go (DiffTest struct and RunDiffTests harness)\n      - cmd/cat/cat_test.go (reference pattern for TestMain, normalizeProgramName, and differential test structure)\n      - cmd/sponge/sponge_test.go (reference pattern for differential test structure with file-output utilities)\n\n    files:\n      - path: cmd/du/du_test.go\n        action: create\n        note: Differential tests for du core directory traversal and output format\n\n    requirements:\n      - id: R1\n        text: \"Test prd009-du R1.1: du recursively traverses each directory argument and prints per-directory subtotals in depth-first order (children printed before parents)\"\n      - id: R2\n        text: \"Test prd009-du R1.2: sizes are displayed in 1024-byte block units, converted from 512-byte raw blocks via ceiling division ((rawBlocks + 1) / 2)\"\n      - id: R3\n        text: \"Test prd009-du R1.3: each output line has the format SIZE<TAB>PATH with one line per directory in default mode\"\n      - id: R4\n        text: \"Test prd009-du R1.5: multiple command-line arguments are processed independently in command-line order; when no arguments are given, du defaults to \\\".\\\"\"\n      - id: R5\n        text: \"Implement a TestMain function that builds the Go du binary into a temporary directory and locates the GNU reference binary via exec.LookPath(\\\"gdu\\\"), following the pattern established in cmd/cat/cat_test.go\"\n      - id: R6\n        text: \"Include a normalizeProgramName helper that replaces \\\"gdu: \\\" with \\\"du: \\\" in stderr output so error messages from the GNU reference and Go binary can be compared byte-for-byte\"\n\n    design_decisions:\n      - id: D1\n        text: \"Follow the TestMain pattern from cmd/cat/cat_test.go: build Go binary with go build -o into a temp directory, locate reference binary via exec.LookPath(\\\"gdu\\\"), skip all tests with os.Exit(0) if gdu is not found on PATH\"\n      - id: D2\n        text: \"Use pkg/testutils.RunDiffTests for all comparisons -- do not manually compare stdout, stderr, or exit code. Pass normalizeProgramName via the DiffTest.Normalize field on tests that trigger stderr output\"\n      - id: D3\n        text: \"Create temporary directory structures with known file content in each test function. Use t.TempDir() for isolation so tests can run in parallel. Populate files with deterministic content to produce stable block counts for differential comparison\"\n      - id: D4\n        text: \"The test file declares package main (same package as cmd/du/main.go) and uses t.Parallel() at the top level of each test function, matching the convention in cmd/cat/cat_test.go\"\n\n    acceptance_criteria:\n      - id: AC1\n        text: cmd/du/du_test.go compiles without errors via go vet ./cmd/du/...\n      - id: AC2\n        text: \"go test ./cmd/du/... passes all differential tests when gdu (GNU du from Homebrew coreutils) is installed on PATH\"\n      - id: AC3\n        text: Tests cover at minimum -- single flat directory, nested directory hierarchy (at least two levels deep), non-directory file argument, multiple arguments in one invocation, and default argument (no args, defaults to \".\")\n      - id: AC4\n        text: Test file declares package main and includes a TestMain function that builds the Go binary and locates gdu, following the pattern from cmd/cat/cat_test.go\n      - id: AC5\n        text: All top-level test functions call t.Parallel() and use t.TempDir() for isolated directory structures\n      - id: AC6\n        text: \"The normalizeProgramName function is applied to stderr via DiffTest.Normalize on every test case that expects stderr output, ensuring \\\"gdu: \\\" and \\\"du: \\\" are compared identically\"\n```"}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":81323,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8129,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"78efe284-a8d4-43a5-bb9f-f328cfcae0b3","uuid":"7e18400d-0316-4372-938a-000000000001"}
